body:
## Dependabot Security Alerts

The following vulnerabilities were identified in the master branch:

```
[
  {
    "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/cccteam/spxscan/dependabot/alerts/18",
    "dependency": {
      "package": {
        "ecosystem": "go",
        "name": "google.golang.org/grpc"
      },
      "manifest_path": "go.mod",
      "scope": "runtime",
      "relationship": "transitive"
    },
    "summary": "gRPC-Go xDS servers: Denial of Service (DoS) via crash due to missing `:authority` and `Host` headers",
    "severity": "high",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-09T17:42:55Z"
  }
]
```

The first sample in `README.md` does not match what the code does, on two counts.

```csharp
var parser = new Parser();
var parsedArguments = parser.Parse("123 45.67 hello world --key=value");

Assert.Equal(3, parsedArguments.StringArguments.Count);
Assert.Equal("value", parsedArguments.NamedArguments["key"]);
```

1. **The key includes its prefix.** `TryParseNamedArgument` splits at the separator and keeps everything to its left, so the key is `"--key"`, not `"key"`. `NamedArguments["key"]` throws `KeyNotFoundException`.
2. **The string count is wrong.** `hello` and `world` are the only string arguments, so it is 2, not 3. The equivalent real test, `TestParser.Test_ArgumentVariety`, asserts 2.

So someone copying the first thing they see in the README gets an exception.

The same document should probably also say plainly that the prefix is retained, since it is the first thing that surprises a reader, and point at the `"--"`-as-argument-separator idiom as the way to strip it. That idiom has its own sharp edge worth mentioning: once `"-"` or `"--"` is an argument separator, hyphens inside values split too, so `--branch:release-1.2` comes apart.

Worth a pass over the other three samples at the same time to check them against the tests.
